WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.399
Path traversal risk often appears around
workflows that move files across

2
00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:08.379
boundaries: uploads, downloads, archive
extraction, exported reports,

3
00:00:08.459 --> 00:00:11.491
generated logs, cached data, and
user-specific storage.

4
00:00:11.571 --> 00:00:15.099
These workflows combine external names,
internal storage policy,

5
00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:18.522
and file operations, so they need
especially clear boundaries.

6
00:00:18.602 --> 00:00:22.563
Uploaded filenames should usually be
treated as labels, not destinations.

7
00:00:22.643 --> 00:00:25.985
A user-supplied name may be useful for
display or audit context,

8
00:00:26.065 --> 00:00:30.398
but the application should choose the
storage root and the controlled stored name.

9
00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:33.944
This avoids letting the external name
decide where the file lives.

10
00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:37.738
Archive entry names and metadata-derived
names need the same caution.

11
00:00:37.818 --> 00:00:39.860
An archive, image, document, package,

12
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.478
or export can contain names that look like
paths.

13
00:00:42.558 --> 00:00:45.838
Those names come from outside the
application trust boundary.

14
00:00:45.918 --> 00:00:49.941
Before extraction or processing, the
application should validate the name,

15
00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:52.683
construct the destination under an
approved root,

16
00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:55.486
and confirm the result remains in the
intended area.

17
00:00:55.566 --> 00:00:59.156
Generated output paths should also follow
controlled naming rules.

18
00:00:59.236 --> 00:01:01.588
It is easy to assume generated names are
safe,

19
00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:05.629
but they may include user labels,
timestamps, identifiers, report titles,

20
00:01:05.709 --> 00:01:06.761
or database fields.

21
00:01:06.841 --> 00:01:09.441
If any component comes from outside
trusted code,

22
00:01:09.521 --> 00:01:12.120
validate or transform it before using it
in a path.

23
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.719
Per-user and per-tenant storage should
enforce separation through directory design

24
00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:17.789
and access checks.

25
00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:21.521
A user identifier should not become an
arbitrary directory selector.

26
00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:24.634
Cleanup routines deserve the same
attention as creation.

27
00:01:24.714 --> 00:01:28.860
They should remove only files managed by
the workflow and should not expand into

28
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.540
broader directories because of unchecked
names.
